{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Mass.PalindromicInvolutionLift"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_fibre_at_zero_coeffs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_fibre_at_zero_coeffs","kind":"theorem","line":277,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L277-L281","doc":"**The colour cubic is not degenerate**, so the previous theorem bites: its\nfibre over `z = 0` has all three non-leading coefficients equal to `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.no_scaling_survives_of_one_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.no_scaling_survives_of_one_lt","kind":"theorem","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L263-L275","doc":"**The substrate case.** The gauge scalars are `q^ℤ` and the substrate modulus\nis real with `q₀ > 1`, so every nontrivial gauge scalar is a real `t > 1`; no such\n`t` leaves any non-leading coefficient of a monic cubic alive. Stated for a\ngeneral real `t > 1` so that no numerical value enters the Lean. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_weighted_rigid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_weighted_rigid","kind":"theorem","line":226,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L226-L259","doc":"**Joint rigidity: fibre scaling AND base dilation together.** If some pair\n`(λ, μ)` of scalings — `X ↦ λ·X` in the fibre, `z ↦ μ·z` on the base — sends the\ncolour cubic to a constant multiple of itself, then all three of `λ, μ, ν` are\n`1`. This is the statement that closes the *combined* move, which neither\n#6769 (base only) nor `eq_one_of_gauge_scaling_fixes_colorCubic` (fibre only) reaches\non its own.\n\nFour evaluations: `(0,0)` fixes `ν`, then `(±1, 0)` fix `λ`, then `(±1, 1)`\ntogether with `(0,1)` fix `μ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.eq_one_of_gauge_scaling_fixes_colorCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.eq_one_of_gauge_scaling_fixes_colorCubic","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L212-L224","doc":"**The colour cubic, sharply.** If the gauge scaling by `t` fixes the colour\ncubic then `t = 1`. Only the fibre over `z = 0` is used — it is `X³+X²+X+1`, all\nthree non-leading coefficients equal to `1`, so no weight can be absorbed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.coeffs_vanish_of_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.coeffs_vanish_of_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L181-L210","doc":"**No monic cubic with a non-leading coefficient survives the gauge scaling.**\nIf the scalar `t` is not a root of `X³ − 1` and not a root of `X² − 1` and is not\n`1`, stability forces `a₂ = a₁ = a₀ = 0` — the cubic degenerates to `X³ = 0`.\n\nSo a Seiberg–Witten spectral cubic is a gauge invariant of a rank-3 q-difference\nmodule only in the degenerate case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.weight_relations","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.weight_relations","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L163-L179","doc":"**The weight relations.** If a monic cubic `X³ + a₂X² + a₁X + a₀` is stable\nunder the gauge scaling `X ↦ t·X` (up to the leading factor `t³`), then each\nnon-leading coefficient is annihilated by its own weight defect.\n\nThree evaluations do it: `X = 0` gives the constant relation, and the sum and\ndifference of `X = ±1` separate the quadratic from the linear one. Characteristic\nzero is used only to halve those two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_scale_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.colorCubic_scale_eq","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L148-L155","doc":"**The gauged cubic IS the rescaled curve.** Scaling the spectral coordinate by\n`t` and renormalising by the leading `t³` is the same as reweighting the\ncoefficients by `t⁻¹`. This is what makes `gaugedColorCubic` the right object\nrather than an ansatz. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.scalar_gauge_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.scalar_gauge_factor","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L139-L146","doc":"**The scalar gauge factor is a power of `q`.** DVRSZ eq. 2.4.1 with the gauge\nmatrix `F = z^k·1` and `σ_q : z ↦ q·z` gives `B = (σ_q F)⁻¹ A F = q^{-k}·A`. So\nthe scalars reachable by a monomial gauge are exactly `q^ℤ`, and a spectral curve\ncan be a gauge invariant only if it survives every `X ↦ q^k·X`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.gaugedColorCubic_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.gaugedColorCubic_one","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L129-L131","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.gaugedColorCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.gaugedColorCubic","kind":"def","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L121-L127","doc":"The **gauged colour cubic** — the colour cubic with the weights its\ncoefficients acquire under the gauge scaling by `t`. At `t = 1` it is the colour\ncubic (`gaugedColorCubic_one`); `colorCubic_scale_eq` shows it is exactly the\nrescaled curve, so the weights are read off rather than posited. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.charCubic_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QGaugeSpectralRigidity.charCubic_smul","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QGaugeSpectralRigidity.lean#L109-L119","doc":"**Scaling the system matrix scales the spectral coefficients by weight.**\nIf a rank-3 system matrix has spectral roots `r₁, r₂, r₃`, then scaling it by `t`\nsends its characteristic cubic to the one whose `X^{3−k}` coefficient carries an\nextra `t^k`. This is the whole mechanism: the gauge `F = z^k·1` multiplies `A` by\na scalar (`scalar_gauge_factor`), and a scalar multiple moves the curve. ","signatureChunk":0}]}